  "request": {
    "spec": "Add a structured address form: the request may provide 'addressLine1', an optional 'addressLine2', 'city' and 'postcode'. Prefer the structured fields when present; the flat 'address' input remains accepted for backward compatibility. An owner is valid when it supplies an address in EITHER form (a non-blank 'addressLine1', or the flat 'address'), plus city; the request contract stays backward-compatible so earlier minimal owners are still accepted. This changes existing behaviour: normalization applies to whichever address fields are supplied, and everything that reads the address (the household hash, postcode validation and locality) uses the structured fields when present, falling back to the flat address. Also return a composed 'address' string equal to the normalized addressLine1, with a single space and the normalized addressLine2 appended when addressLine2 is present.",
    "prompt": "Implement the following change to the application so that it fully satisfies the\nspecification and all existing and new tests pass.\n\nWrite it to keep structural impact low. Structural impact is a measurable cost, not a\nvague goal. For every function you change, the cost is:\n    cost = max(WMC_other, 1) * CC * max(1, changed_lines)\nCC is that function's cyclomatic complexity. WMC_other is the summed cyclomatic\ncomplexity of the OTHER methods in its class, which is the surrounding context you must\nhold to edit it safely. changed_lines is how many of its lines you add or edit. The costs\nare summed over every function you change, then multiplied by the number of files you\ntouch. So the biggest lever is WMC_other: a method inside a large class pays for the whole\nclass. Do not grow one method or one class. Add behaviour as small, cohesive units, each\nwith little surrounding complexity, and keep every function you change simple.\n\nRun the test suite and make it green before finishing.\n\nAdd a structured address form: the request may provide 'addressLine1', an optional 'addressLine2', 'city' and 'postcode'. Prefer the structured fields when present; the flat 'address' input remains accepted for backward compatibility. An owner is valid when it supplies an address in EITHER form (a non-blank 'addressLine1', or the flat 'address'), plus city; the request contract stays backward-compatible so earlier minimal owners are still accepted. This changes existing behaviour: normalization applies to whichever address fields are supplied, and everything that reads the address (the household hash, postcode validation and locality) uses the structured fields when present, falling back to the flat address. Also return a composed 'address' string equal to the normalized addressLine1, with a single space and the normalized addressLine2 appended when addressLine2 is present.\n"
  },
